VERDICT: FAIL — 1 blocking finding (B1), 5 advisories.

== B1 (BLOCKING) guard fails OPEN on a git error that exits with a status
Brief (Ownership function): "If git is missing or fails in steps 2-4, the
helper refuses and says so. It never silently allows, because a guard that
fails open is how W-026 round 5 happened." recordOwnerRefusal's own docstring
says the same. The implementation does not do this: runGit sets
`broken: true` only when the spawn fails (ENOENT/timeout/signal). Any
numeric exit status is treated as the benign "no" answer:
  - step 2  `symbolic-ref -q HEAD`: exit 128 -> headRef undefined, treated
            as detached -> falls to step 4
  - step 4  `show-ref --verify --quiet`: exit 128 -> `code !== 0`, treated
            as "branch absent" -> step 5 ALLOW
Both commands' documented benign negative is exit 1; any other status is
a failure.
Reproduced (real repo, record tracked, opus/W-9 exists, HEAD=master):
  append one garbage line to .git/packed-refs
  git -C studio ls-files --error-unmatch -- opera/W-9.md  -> exit 0 (step 1 passes)
  git symbolic-ref -q HEAD                                -> exit 128
  git show-ref --verify --quiet refs/heads/opus/W-9       -> exit 128
  recordOwnerRefusal(<repo>/studio, "W-9")                -> ALLOW   (defect)
  restore packed-refs, same call                          -> REFUSE ("owned by opus/W-9 ... D-021")
So a trunk write goes through while the opus branch exists. That is the
exact conflict class D-021 exists to kill, and the brief forbids it.
Class: "non-zero git exit status conflated with the benign negative
answer". Searched every runGit call site in writes.ts (4: ls-files,
symbolic-ref, show-ref, rev-parse). Instances: 2, step 2 and step 4.
Step 1's ls-files allowing on any non-zero is the brief's explicit design,
so not an instance. describeDetached only builds message text, so not an
instance.
Coverage gap on the same path: no test reaches gitFailureRefusal. Grep of
packages/ and apps/ for its text or name finds nothing outside writes.ts.
Fix: treat exit 1 as the negative answer and every other non-zero as
broken at both call sites. Add one real-repo block that corrupts
packed-refs to induce the 128 and asserts exit 2 with the git-failure
message and a byte-identical record.

== Advisories (non-blocking; A1 is cheap enough to fold into the B1 round)
A1 Guard placement. Brief: "right after the command has resolved and
   existence-checked the opus path". Five of seven callers put it after
   later state/spec/gate checks: ready (after state and spec), done (after
   state and gates), halt (after state), handoff (after --stage), greenlight
   (after state). review and verify comply. The D-021 invariant still
   holds, because only reads come before the guard (verified by reading
   each). But in this officina the trunk shows in-flight opera as
   `greenlit` (D-021 Consequence), so `done`/`close` from the trunk
   reports "not building, verifying or review (state: greenlit)" instead
   of the D-021 ownership message. `done` with missing gates exits 1, not 2.
   Class: 5 instances.
A2 Step-3 message preamble: "opera/X.md is owned by opus/X while that
   branch exists" is printed when opus/X does NOT exist (behaviour 5's
   fixture). Only the tail ("carries only Y's record") is true. 1 instance.
A3 Vacuous assertions, which pass with no guard at all (seen when
   reproducing reds at e355b00 source):
   b3 "handoff appends no event" (handoff emits no event);
   b4 "record byte-identical" and b4 "no new file under studio/ci/" (the
   stub pipeline returns {}, so unguarded verify writes nothing).
   Class: 3 instances. Every block still has biting assertions (mutations
   below). A stub that returns one passed gate would make b4's two bite.
A4 `ci --opus` from the trunk runs every CI step before verify's refusal.
   Nothing is written, but a whole CI run is wasted. Outside the brief's
   text.
A5 The close and `ci --opus` inheritance holds by construction
   (executeClose -> runDone, ci -> runVerify). No test covers it.

